Scene & Origin Lore
In 1987, two kilometers down Shaft Four of the Lubin copper basin, miners working brutal twelve-hour shifts discovered that striking the galvanized high-pressure ventilation tubing produced crystalline, tuned overtones that cut cleanly through the deafening roar of heavy excavation. To combat sensory exhaustion, a rebellious night-shift crew began sneaking in battery-powered gear—a modified Roland TR-808, a pocket cassette four-track, and a Yamaha DX7—tapping into the air ducts to jam during rest pauses. When off-shift, these workers, nicknamed haftiarze ("seam-tailors"), ascended to the surface and climbed onto the gravel tar-paper roofs of industrial tenement blocks, setting up cheap telescopes to gaze at the night sky they were starved of all day. Subculture fashion merged subterranean necessity with romantic leisure: oversized moleskin pit-jackets paired with crushed plum-velvet scarves, brass welding goggles fitted with amber glass for nocturnal stargazing, and wool beanies dusted with chalky copper residue. Their listening rituals, known as Pod-Cisza ("Under-Silence"), brought dozens of workers to drafty rooftops to recline on thick acoustic blast-curtains, sipping chicory coffee laced with condensed milk while cassette players hummed with deep, subterranean jazz.

